<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dubricius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Dyfrig, Saint Dubricius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dubricius, in Welsh Dyfrig, was a 6th-century British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and saint active in Ergyng, in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Herefordshire. Later tradition made him archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llandaff">Llandaff</a> and the bishop who crowned King Arthur, but the earliest sources show him as a founder of churches in the Wye valley. His relics were moved to Llandaff in 1120.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dubricius.jpg" alt="Dubricius" width="320" height="524" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hchc2009 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castell_Coch_stained_glass_panel_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
